<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Benares State</h1><p class="st-meta">1737 CE – 1949 CE (approx.) · capital Ramnagar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The kingdom of the Maharajas of Benares at Ramnagar fort, famous for its Ramlila and for Chait Singh's revolt against Warren Hastings.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mansa-state">Mansa</a> Ram, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhumihar">Bhumihar</a> landholder, obtained the zamindari of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Benares</a> around 1737 under the Nawab of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balwant-singh-of-benares">Balwant Singh</a> built a semi-independent principality and the fort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramnagar-varanasi">Ramnagar</a> across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> from Varanasi. In 1775 the Nawab ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over the area to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chait-singh">Chait Singh's</a> resistance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warren-hastings">Warren Hastings's</a> heavy demands led to his rising and flight in 1781, a key charge in Hastings's later impeachment. The family kept large estates as zamindars, and in 1911 part of these lands, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramnagar-varanasi">Ramnagar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhadohi">Bhadohi</a>, was made a princely state. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharajas</a> were patrons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> learning, and the month-long <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramnagar-ramlila">Ramnagar Ramlila</a> they sponsor remains famous.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vibhuti-narayan-singh">Vibhuti Narayan Singh</a> acceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Benares</a> State was merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a> in 1949. The family still hosts the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramlila">Ramlila</a> and keeps a museum at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramnagar-varanasi">Ramnagar</a> fort.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/benares-state.jpg" alt="Benares State" width="320" height="175"><figcaption>Image: Great Britain India Office · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Benares_State-Imperial_Gazetteer_of_India.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1737 CE – 1949 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ramnagar</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Balwant Singh, Chait Singh, Vibhuti Narayan Singh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hindi, Bhojpuri</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Banaras State, Kashi State</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1843&amp;focus=benares-state" aria-label="Benares State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6878 1248 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Benares State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7198 1504L7364 1601M7198 1504L7364 1602M7198 1504L7018 1386"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7364 1601h0M7364 1602h0M7018 1386h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7198 1504h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7198 1504h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1843&amp;focus=benares-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
